steps.body:
`while (condition) { ... }` checks the condition, runs the body if it is `true`, then checks again. It stops the first time the condition is `false`. The body must move toward that point, usually by updating the loop variable. Forget the update and the loop never ends.

```java
int count = 3;
while (count > 0) {
    System.out.println(count);
    count--; // the update
}
```

The classic use is walking the digits of a number. `n % 10` is the last digit and `n /= 10` drops it. Loop until nothing is left:

```java
int temp = 9475;
while (temp > 0) {
    int digit = temp % 10; // 5, then 7, 4, 9
    temp /= 10;            // 947, then 94, 9, 0
}
```

To build a number from digits, shift left and add: `rev = rev * 10 + digit`.

Gotchas: work on a copy (`temp`) when you still need the original. Reversing drops trailing zeros (1200 becomes 21) and can overflow `int` for big values. A `temp > 0` condition skips negatives and treats `0` as having no digits.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 9475;

        int sum = 0;
        int temp = n;
        while (temp > 0) {
            sum += temp % 10;
            temp /= 10;
        }
        System.out.println("Digit sum of " + n + ": " + sum);

        int rev = 0;
        temp = n;
        while (temp > 0) {
            int digit = temp % 10;
            rev = rev * 10 + digit;
            temp /= 10;
        }
        System.out.println("Reversed: " + rev);
    }
}

