        int n = 1203004;

        int count = 0;
        int rev = 0;
        int temp = n;
        while (temp > 0) {
            count++;
            temp /= 10;
        }

        temp = n;
        while (temp > 0) {
            int digit = temp % 10;
            rev = rev * 10 + digit;
            temp /= 10;
        }

        System.out.println("Digits in " + n + ": " + count);
        System.out.println("Reversed: " + rev);
    }
}

steps.body:
`do { ... } while (condition);` runs the body first and checks the condition after. So the body always runs at least once. Note the semicolon after the closing parenthesis: leaving it out is a compile error.

```java
int value = 100;
do {
    System.out.println(value);
    value /= 2;
} while (value >= 5);
```

A plain `while` checks first. If its condition starts `false`, the body runs zero times. That is the only difference between the two:

| Loop | Checks | Minimum runs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `while` | before the body | 0 |
| `do-while` | after the body | 1 |

Use `do-while` when the work must happen once before there is anything to test. Counting digits is a classic case: `while (temp > 0)` says 0 has zero digits, but a `do-while` with the same body counts it as one. Most loops are still plain `while` or `for`.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int value = 100;
        do {
            System.out.println(value);
            value /= 2;
        } while (value >= 5);
        System.out.println("Stopped at " + value);

        int start = 3;
        do {
            System.out.println("do-while: " + start);
            start /= 2;
        } while (start >= 5);

        start = 3;
        while (start >= 5) {
            System.out.println("while: " + start);
            start /= 2;
        }
        System.out.println("Done");
    }
}

