    }
}

steps.body:
Sometimes the condition is easier to say as *when to stop* than as when to keep going. Write `while (true)` and leave with `break`:

```java
int steps = 0;
while (true) {
    if (n == 1) {
        break; // the exit
    }
    // ...one step of work...
    steps++;
}
```

`break` jumps to the first line after the loop. Counting steps is the usual pattern: increment a counter on every pass and print it after the loop.

Gotchas: every `while (true)` needs a `break` that some path will reach, or the program hangs until the sandbox kills it. As with for loops, a `break` inside a `switch` statement only leaves the `switch`, not the loop around it. An exit test at the top could also be the loop condition, as in `while (n != 1)`. `while (true)` earns its place when the exit sits in the middle of the body or there is more than one exit.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 27;
        int steps = 0;
        int peak = n;
        while (true) {
            if (n == 1) {
                break;
            }
            if (n % 2 == 0) {
                n /= 2;
            } else {
                n = 3 * n + 1;
            }
            steps++;
            if (n > peak) {
                peak = n;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Steps from 27 to 1: " + steps);
        System.out.println("Highest value: " + peak);
    }
}

